---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="How scoring works" description="A plain-language guide to the lightweight GameBench 0.6 workflow.">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">GameBench 0.6</span>
    <h1>Four games, one development attempt, one machine evaluation.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The score comes only from public browser contracts. Human review is optional
      and never changes the ranking.
    </p>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="grid cols-3">
        <article class="card"><span class="pill">2048</span><h3>Discrete rules</h3><p>Grid state, merges, reset behavior, seed handling, and keyboard input.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><span class="pill">Minesweeper</span><h3>Hidden random state</h3><p>Mine counts, expansion, flags, pointer input, and deterministic boards.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><span class="pill">2D Parking</span><h3>Continuous simulation</h3><p>Motion, steering, collision, geometry, and stable controlled time.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><span class="pill">Texas Hold'em</span><h3>Complex rules</h3><p>Six seats, betting rounds, hand ranking, all-ins, side pots, and chip conservation.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Prompt to score</span><h2>Every step has one job.</h2></div></div>
      <div class="grid cols-3 how-grid">
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>1. Real preflight</h3><p>Before paying for a model, GameBench proves that install, build, preview, Chromium, ports, and the bridge work on the actual host.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>2. One Agent attempt</h3><p>Each task starts clean and calls the coding Agent exactly once. A timeout freezes the code already delivered; development is never retried automatically.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>3. Frozen source</h3><p>The delivered project is archived and hashed before testing, so evaluation cannot silently repair or replace it.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>4. One clean evaluation</h3><p>The archive is unpacked into a new directory, installed, built, served, and checked with the canonical seed <code>104729</code>.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>5. Equal-weight Build score</h3><p>Each game is worth 100 points. The headline score is the simple average of all four games, with no hidden weights or human judge.</p></article>
        <article class="card how-card"><h3>6. Local integrity check</h3><p>Hashes, task identity, score arithmetic, coverage, and artifacts are checked without a second build or verifier.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>What “Official” means</h2>
      <p>An Official 0.6 result is a complete, clean-tree run produced by the project’s canonical runner and committed by the repository maintainers. It is not a claim of independent third-party reproduction.</p>
      <p>Infrastructure errors never become model zeroes. The same frozen source may be evaluated again after a host failure, but the Agent does not receive another development attempt.</p>
      <p>GameBench 0.6 deliberately has no Docker requirement, verifier, Reproduce board, Core composite, or three-seed loop.</p>

      <h2>How to interpret score differences</h2>
      <p>Each result is one observed delivery per task, not an estimate of average model performance. Four selected games and their public cases do not provide a confidence interval or establish that a small score gap is statistically significant. Read the task scores and build-gate failures alongside the average.</p>
      <p>The measured unit is the configured Agent, provider, and model system. One invocation and the same time limit do not imply equal token use, inference compute, or cost. Cross-Campaign comparisons are descriptive and may include execution-date, provider, harness, and host effects.</p>
      <p>Passing public state and input checks does not establish visual quality, fun, complete rule coverage, or generalization to arbitrary seeds. Re-testing frozen code checks evaluator repeatability, not independent Agent reliability.</p>

      <details class="audit">
        <summary>Deterministic browser contract</summary>
        <div class="audit-body">
          <p>Cases combine native keyboard and pointer events with a public bridge for reset, controlled time, actions, and schema-validated snapshots. One physical evaluation contains multiple explicit deterministic scenarios.</p>
          <p>Build, preview, bridge availability, and the initial snapshot are hard gates. Individual game-rule failures otherwise lose only their declared points.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
